---
interface Tab { key: string; title: string; body: string[]; list?: string[]; repo?: string; }
interface Props { lang: 'zh' | 'en'; }
const { lang } = Astro.props;
const zh = lang === 'zh';

const tabs: Tab[] = zh ? [
  {
    key: 'overview',
    title: '项目概览',
    body: [
      '一个开源的求职自动化工具——不是我从零搭起来的项目，但架构和功能里有不小一部分出自我手。截至目前，76 个提交的 PR 被合并，涉及评分逻辑、边界情况处理、以及几套完整的子系统设计。我是贡献者，不是原作者。',
    ],
    repo: 'https://github.com/santifer/career-ops',
  },
  {
    key: 'reverse-ats',
    title: 'Reverse ATS 扫描器',
    body: [
      '这是我合并的第一个 PR（2026年6月）。起因很朴素：career-ops 原本的 scan.mjs 要求你手动在 portals.yml 里维护一份公司清单，我懒得手动维护这份清单，就把方向倒过来了——不再是"我告诉系统要查哪些公司"，是让系统反向遍历各家 ATS（Greenhouse、Lever、Ashby、Workday）公开的公司目录，自动挖出匹配你 title/location 过滤条件的新职位，不需要手动维护任何公司名单，也不花一分 LLM token。',
      '技术上新增了 providers/workday.mjs，对接 Workday 公开的 CXS 职位接口（分页 POST 请求），能自动从 careers_url 识别是不是 Workday 站点；同时复用了 scan.mjs 现有的过滤、去重、写入逻辑，没有另起一套。没有可用发布日期的职位会被跳过——反向扫描的意义就在于只抓新鲜职位，抓到旧的没有用。',
    ],
    repo: 'https://github.com/santifer/career-ops/pull/746',
  },
  {
    key: 'jurisdiction',
    title: 'Jurisdiction Compliance 架构',
    body: [
      '这是我最满意的部分——整个架构是我设计的，起点也不是抽象的兴趣，是我自己的真实遭遇：我被雇主用工身份误分类过，走了 CRA 的正式裁定程序，最终裁定确认我是雇员身份，不是对方一直坚持的独立合同工。整个架构就是从"我自己踩过这个坑"直接长出来的，不是先有技术方案再找场景套。',
      '起点是发现 career-ops 读取的东西（招聘文案、申请表单、面试对话、offer 文件、流程时间线）恰好是各地劳动法在监管的对象：安大略 2026 年的招聘公示新规、美国各州的薪资历史禁令、欧盟与日本的公平招聘框架。于是我把它做成一条通用检测流程：跟候选人所在司法辖区做比对，只提醒、不代劳、不判定"雇主违法"。',
      '架构定下了一份共享契约，后续每个信号都遵守：（1）检测逻辑通用，只有数据行本地化——加拿大只是最常见的第一颗种子，不是"专属功能"；（2）没有可引用法律来源和生效日期的条目不能进表；（3）措辞只陈述可验证的事实（"这个薪资区间宽 9 万，O. Reg. 476/24 规定上限 5 万"），从不断言"这家雇主违法"；（4）只提醒、人在回路，不自动拦截、不自动填表、不自动改用户文件；（5）"存在性"信号可独立触发，"缺失性"信号只能作为佐证——因为聚合平台经常整段抹掉合规区块。',
      '目前已合并 11 个信号成员：拒信延迟、禁止内容（"加拿大经验"要求 / 薪资历史询问）、薪资透明度披露、最低工资地板、竞业限制条款说明、低于法定条款检查、受保护事项面试提问、移民身份要求越界、用工身份误分类、中介牌照核验，外加一个让所有表格保持时效性的"过期校验器"。每个信号都落在 career-ops 现有模式文件的对应位置——招聘文案/申请表进 Block G，offer 文件进 offer-prep，面试对话进 interview-redflag，流程时间线进 followup。',
    ],
    repo: 'https://github.com/santifer/career-ops/issues/2026',
  },
  {
    key: 'sot',
    title: 'Source-of-Truth 分层设计',
    body: [
      '这个也是我设计的：把 career-ops 原本"一刀切"的可信文件列表拆成两层。cv.md、article-digest.md、config/profile.yml 这些用户亲手写的文件是"主层"，完全可信；story-bank.md 这类"衍生层"文件——通常是从过往面试准备文档里提炼出来的——只给予表达/措辞层面的信任，不能单独作为事实来源。',
      '起因是发现了一条真实可复现的漏洞链：JD 措辞被写进某次面试准备文档 → 被吸收进 story-bank.md 变成一条独立故事 → 被当成跟 cv.md 同等可信的事实 → 最终出现在简历条目或求职信里 → 每复用一次就再漂移一点。修复方案本身遵循"只读不改、需要确认才能升级"的原则——衍生文件永远不会被脚本自动提权为主层事实，必须经过一次显式确认。这条不变式被维护者专门点名认可。',
    ],
    repo: 'https://github.com/santifer/career-ops/pull/2948',
  },
  {
    key: 'sqlite',
    title: 'SQLite 索引层设计',
    body: [
      '这是我自己提的 RFC，也是我实现的：给求职追踪器（当时是一份纯 Markdown 表格）加一层 SQLite 派生索引。起因是自己的真实数据——两个月内积累到 934 条记录后，校验脚本报出 422 个错误，全是"多写入者 + 没有 schema"这种结构性问题的产物，不是用户手误：编码乱码、分数串到状态列里、ID 缺失或重复、日期格式错乱。',
      '设计上最关键的一步是把原本提议的方案倒过来：不是让数据库直接取代 Markdown 成为唯一真相来源，而是先做"纯派生索引"——Markdown 继续是唯一真相来源，数据库只是从它重建出来的只读镜像，删掉随时可以重建，不会丢任何东西。数据库替代 Markdown 成为主存储，被明确划成后续一个独立的、用户自己选择开启的阶段，不是默认行为。这个提案被维护者采纳，"顺序倒过来"就是照我的方案定的。',
      '实现只用了 Node 22.5+ 内置的 SQLite，没引入新依赖；round-trip 测试保证 Markdown → 数据库 → 导出回 Markdown 完全一致；用真实的 934 条数据验证过，一次同步就报出全部 422 个错误分类且原文件分毫未动。',
    ],
    repo: 'https://github.com/santifer/career-ops/pull/919',
  },
] : [
  {
    key: 'overview',
    title: 'Overview',
    body: [
      "An open-source job-search automation tool — not a project I built from zero, but a fair share of its architecture and features are mine. 76 merged PRs to date, spanning scoring logic, edge-case handling, and a few full subsystem designs. I'm a contributor, not the original author.",
    ],
    repo: 'https://github.com/santifer/career-ops',
  },
  {
    key: 'reverse-ats',
    title: 'Reverse ATS Scanner',
    body: [
      "This was my first merged PR (June 2026). The reason was plain laziness: career-ops's original scan.mjs required manually maintaining a company list in portals.yml, and I didn't want to keep curating that list by hand — so I flipped the direction. Instead of telling the system which companies to check, the system now walks the public company directories of each ATS (Greenhouse, Lever, Ashby, Workday) itself and surfaces new postings matching your title/location filters — no manual company list, zero LLM tokens spent.",
      'Technically this added providers/workday.mjs, hitting Workday\'s public CXS jobs endpoint (paginated POST requests), auto-detecting Workday sites from the careers_url. It reuses scan.mjs\'s existing filter/dedup/write logic rather than building a separate pipeline. Postings without a usable publish date get skipped — the whole point of a reverse scan is surfacing fresh postings, not old ones.',
    ],
    repo: 'https://github.com/santifer/career-ops/pull/746',
  },
  {
    key: 'jurisdiction',
    title: 'Jurisdiction Compliance Architecture',
    body: [
      "This is the part I'm proudest of — I designed the whole architecture, and it didn't start as an abstract interest. It started with my own case: I was misclassified by an employer, went through CRA's formal ruling process, and the ruling confirmed I was an employee, not the independent contractor the employer had insisted on. The whole architecture grew directly out of a hole I'd fallen into myself, not a technical idea looking for a use case afterward.",
      "It started from noticing that what career-ops already reads (posting text, application forms, interview conversations, offer documents, process timelines) is exactly what local employment law regulates: Ontario's 2026 posting-disclosure rules, US state salary-history bans, EU and Japan fair-hiring frameworks. So I built it as one generic check pipeline: compare against the candidate's own jurisdiction, warn only, never assert an employer broke the law.",
      'The architecture is pinned by a shared contract every signal follows: (1) the check logic is universal, only the data rows are local — Canada is just the most frequent first seed, not a dedicated feature; (2) no table entry without a citable legal source and an effective date; (3) phrasing states only the verifiable fact ("this range is $90K wide; O. Reg. 476/24 caps covered ranges at $50K"), never "this employer is breaking the law"; (4) warn-only, human-in-the-loop — never blocks, never auto-fills, never auto-edits user files; (5) presence-based signals can fire standalone, absence-based signals are corroborating-only, since aggregators often strip compliance blocks entirely.',
      "11 signal members are merged so far: rejection latency, prohibited content (\"Canadian experience\" requirements / salary-history questions), pay-transparency disclosure, minimum-wage floor, restrictive-covenant notes, sub-statutory terms check, protected-grounds interview questions, immigration-status requirement overreach, employment misclassification, agency licensing check, plus a staleness validator that keeps every table current. Each signal lands in the matching existing mode file — posting/form surfaces go into Block G, offer documents into offer-prep, interview conversations into interview-redflag, process timelines into followup.",
    ],
    repo: 'https://github.com/santifer/career-ops/issues/2026',
  },
  {
    key: 'sot',
    title: 'Source-of-Truth Tiering',
    body: [
      "Also my design: splitting career-ops's previously flat list of trusted files into two tiers. User-authored files like cv.md, article-digest.md, and config/profile.yml form the primary tier, fully trusted. Derived files like story-bank.md — usually distilled from past interview-prep documents — only get narrative/phrasing-level trust, never standalone fact authority.",
      "It came from finding a real, reproducible vulnerability chain: JD-shaped phrasing lands in one interview-prep document → gets absorbed into story-bank.md as a standalone story → gets treated as cv.md-equivalent fact → surfaces in CV bullets or cover letters → drifts a little further with every reuse. The fix itself follows a read-only-by-construction principle — a derived file is never auto-promoted to primary-tier fact by any script; it always requires an explicit confirmation step. The maintainer specifically praised this invariant.",
    ],
    repo: 'https://github.com/santifer/career-ops/pull/2948',
  },
  {
    key: 'sqlite',
    title: 'SQLite Index Layer',
    body: [
      "I proposed this RFC myself, and implemented it: adding a SQLite derived-index layer on top of the application tracker (a plain markdown table at the time). It came from my own real data — after two months of heavy use, the tracker hit 934 rows and the validator reported 422 errors, all structural artifacts of many writers plus no schema, not user typos: encoding corruption, scores stranded in the status column, missing or duplicate IDs, malformed dates.",
      "The key design decision was inverting the originally proposed rollout order: instead of letting the database immediately replace markdown as the single source of truth, ship a pure derived index first — markdown stays the source of truth, the database is just a read-only mirror rebuilt from it, safe to delete and rebuild at any point without losing anything. Flipping the database into the primary store became an explicit, separate, opt-in phase later, never the default. That proposal was adopted — the inverted order is exactly my design.",
      "The implementation uses only Node 22.5+'s built-in SQLite, no new dependency. Round-trip tests guarantee markdown → database → exported markdown is byte-identical. Validated against real data: one sync against the 934-row tracker reported every one of the 422 error classes while leaving the source file untouched.",
    ],
    repo: 'https://github.com/santifer/career-ops/pull/919',
  },
];

const repoLabel = zh ? '在 GitHub 查看 →' : 'View on GitHub →';
---
<div class="ep-layout careerops-ep-layout">
  <div class="ep-tabs">
    {tabs.map((t, i) => (
      <button type="button" class={`ep-tab${i === 0 ? ' active' : ''}`} data-ep={i}>{t.title}</button>
    ))}
  </div>
  <div class="ep-panels">
    {tabs.map((t, i) => (
      <div class={`ep-panel${i === 0 ? ' active' : ''}`} data-ep={i}>
        <h2 class="ep-title">{t.title}</h2>
        {t.body.map((p) => <p class="wk-desc">{p}</p>)}
        {t.repo && <a class="wk-repo" href={t.repo} target="_blank" rel="noopener">{repoLabel}</a>}
      </div>
    ))}
  </div>
</div>

<style>
  .careerops-ep-layout { margin-top:24px; display:grid; grid-template-columns:230px 1fr; gap:36px; align-items:start; }
  .careerops-ep-layout .ep-tabs { display:flex; flex-direction:column; position:sticky; top:24px; }
  .careerops-ep-layout .ep-tab { text-align:left; background:none; border:none; border-left:2px solid var(--line); padding:11px 16px; font:inherit; font-size:.93rem; color:var(--muted); cursor:pointer; line-height:1.4; transition:color .18s ease,border-color .18s ease; }
  .careerops-ep-layout .ep-tab:hover { color:var(--ink); }
  .careerops-ep-layout .ep-tab.active { border-left-color:var(--rose); color:var(--ink); font-weight:500; }
  .careerops-ep-layout .ep-panels { min-width:0; }
  .careerops-ep-layout .ep-panel { display:none; }
  .careerops-ep-layout .ep-panel.active { display:block; }
  .careerops-ep-layout .ep-title { margin:0 0 18px; font-size:clamp(1.6rem,3vw,2.2rem); line-height:1.25; font-weight:500; }
  .wk-desc { color:var(--muted); font-size:.98rem; line-height:1.8; margin:0 0 18px; max-width:700px; }
  .wk-repo { display:inline-block; color:var(--rose); font-size:.92rem; text-decoration:none; margin-top:6px; }
  .wk-repo:hover { text-decoration:underline; }
  .careerops-ep-layout .ep-panel.entering { animation: ep-panel-in .52s cubic-bezier(.2,.7,.2,1) both; }
  @keyframes ep-panel-in { from { opacity:0; transform:translateY(12px); } to { opacity:1; transform:none; } }
  @media (max-width:760px) { .careerops-ep-layout { grid-template-columns:1fr; } .careerops-ep-layout .ep-tabs { flex-direction:row; flex-wrap:wrap; position:static; gap:4px 14px; } .careerops-ep-layout .ep-tab { border-left:none; border-bottom:2px solid var(--line); padding:6px 2px; } .careerops-ep-layout .ep-tab.active { border-bottom-color:var(--rose); } }
  @media (prefers-reduced-motion:reduce) { .careerops-ep-layout .ep-panel.entering { animation:none; } }
</style>

<script is:inline>
  document.addEventListener('click', function (event) {
    var tab = event.target.closest('.careerops-ep-layout .ep-tab');
    if (!tab) return;
    var layout = tab.closest('.careerops-ep-layout');
    var tabs = layout.querySelectorAll('.ep-tab');
    var panels = layout.querySelectorAll('.ep-panel');
    tabs.forEach(function (t) { t.classList.remove('active'); });
    panels.forEach(function (p) { p.classList.remove('active', 'entering'); });
    tab.classList.add('active');
    var panel = layout.querySelector('.ep-panel[data-ep="' + tab.dataset.ep + '"]');
    if (!panel) return;
    panel.classList.add('active');
    window.requestAnimationFrame(function () { panel.classList.add('entering'); });
  });

  document.addEventListener('astro:page-load', function () {
    document.querySelectorAll('.careerops-ep-layout').forEach(function (layout) {
      var tabs = layout.querySelectorAll('.ep-tab');
      var panels = layout.querySelectorAll('.ep-panel');
      if (!tabs.length) return;
      tabs.forEach(function (t) { t.classList.remove('active'); });
      panels.forEach(function (p) { p.classList.remove('active', 'entering'); });
      tabs[0].classList.add('active');
      var panel = layout.querySelector('.ep-panel[data-ep="' + tabs[0].dataset.ep + '"]');
      if (panel) panel.classList.add('active');
    });
  });
</script>
